Remaining failure: SWD wire returns DAP_TRANSFER_ERROR (8)
-----------------------------------------------------------

pyocd's CMSIS-DAP-v2 init runs DAP_Info, DAP_HostStatus, DAP_Connect,
DAP_TransferConfigure, then issues DAP_SWJ_Sequence bursts and a
DAP_SWD_Sequence (the multi-drop TARGETSEL for RP2040), then issues
DAP_TransferBlock to read DPIDR (DP register 0x00). This is the
first SWD-wire transaction. The synthetic responder forwards it to
DAP_SWD_TransferBlock in the vendored CMSIS-DAP source, which calls
SWD_Transfer (port/swd_glue.c) into the io/swd.c engine. Engine
returns SWD_STATUS_PROTOCOL or PARITY_ERR, mapped back to
DAP_TRANSFER_ERROR = 0x08.

Wire trace of the failing exchange:

  EP1 OUT: 06 00 01 00 02
    cmd      = 0x06 (DAP_TransferBlock)
    dap_idx  = 0x00
    count_lo = 0x01
    count_hi = 0x00
    block_request = 0x02 (RnW=1, APnDP=0, A2=0, A3=0 -> read DP[0]=DPIDR)

  EP2 IN  (response): 06 00 00 08
    cmd      = 0x06
    count_lo = 0x00 (zero transfers completed)
    count_hi = 0x00
    response = 0x08 (DAP_TRANSFER_ERROR; SWD parity / protocol fault)

pyocd's _check_response masks ack with 0x07 and gets 0, so it raises
"Unexpected ACK value (0) returned by probe". This is an artefact of
the mask: 0x08 is the protocol-error bit, not an ACK value, but
pyocd's path through _decode_transfer_block_data calls _check_response
on data[3] without checking the protocol-error bit first. The
underlying issue is the SWD wire, not the responder.

Plausible root causes (none of them in scope for this agent):
- DUT VBUS not actually powered. annealage_pod.boot.up() does not call
  power.dut_usb.on(); manually invoking power.dut_usb.on() before
  pyocd reset did not change the result, suggesting either the Pico
  is powered from another rail or the boot sequence needs to enable
  this rail before SWD comes up. Either way, lives in WS-E (boot)
  or hardware bring-up.
- Wiring / level-shifter mismatch. The SWD engine (io/swd.c) drives
  GPIO12 as a DIR strobe for an A-side level translator. If the
  hardware does not have that translator (direct wiring through
  GPIO11 to Pico SWDIO, as the task brief implies), the DIR strobe
  is harmless but the SPI2 3-wire half-duplex direction switching
  on GPIO11 must work cleanly. Live silicon validation needed.
- SWCLK (GPIO10) routing through the SPI2 GPIO matrix vs IOMUX. The
  SWDIO pin is IOMUX-direct (GPIO11 = SPI2 native MOSI); SCLK is
  matrix-routed which adds 2-3 cycles of skew at 10 MHz that may
  push setup/hold past spec for the Pico's SWD slave timing. This
  is mentioned in the swd.c init log line ("iomux=yes" for SWDIO
  but no equivalent for SCLK).
- Pico in BOOTSEL mode, USB boot, or otherwise rejecting SWD. Hold
  BOOTSEL low at power-on -> RP2040 enters USB MSC mode but SWD
  remains active; not a likely cause but easy to rule out by
  releasing BOOTSEL before pyocd reset.

Allowed-but-not-helpful edits explored
---------------------------------------

The task's allowed-edit set covers usbip/, usbhost/, synthetic_device.{c,h},
and moddapprobe.c. None of these can fix the SWD-wire failure:
- usbip/ is below the DAP layer; the URB chain works.
- usbhost/ is irrelevant for the synthetic-CMSIS-DAP path (target=virtual).
- synthetic_device.c forwards verbatim to dap_core_process; any change
  would corrupt the CMSIS-DAP protocol.
- moddapprobe.c is the MP binding only.

Disallowed edits that would be needed to actually fix this:
- src/c_modules/dapprobe/dap_core.c (CMSIS-DAP vendored layer +
  init wiring) - off-limits.
- src/c_modules/dapprobe/io/swd.c, io/swd.h (the SPI2 + dedic_gpio
  SWD engine) - off-limits.
- src/c_modules/dapprobe/port/swd_glue.c (DAP-to-engine adapter) -
  off-limits.
- src/mpy/annealage_pod/boot.py (would need power.dut_usb.on() before
  start_usbip) - off-limits.
- src/boards/ESP32_S3_ANNEALAGE_POD/* (level-shifter pin wiring) -
  explicitly off-limits.

Recommended next session
------------------------

1. WS-D bring-up validation pass. With p30-trace-final.log as the
   reference for what pyocd issues over CMSIS-DAP, hook a logic
   analyser to GPIO10 (SWCLK) and GPIO11 (SWDIO) and capture the
   bytes the SWD engine drives during the failing DAP_TransferBlock.
   Compare the wire output to a known-good Pi Debug Probe driving
   the same RP2040. The mismatch will be in:
     a. SWCLK frequency or duty cycle
     b. SWDIO direction switch timing during the turnaround
     c. The 50-cycle line-reset preceding the read
2. Verify DUT VBUS. Either flash a annealage_pod.boot variant that calls
   power.dut_usb.on() in the boot sequence, or confirm via INA228
   shunt that DUT-USB-VBUS is energised and drawing the expected
   ~70 mA the Pico draws at idle.
3. Once SWD is up, the URB chain is already proven; pyocd reset
   should complete cleanly with no further work on the multiplexer.

